# Every path whose bytes are hashed is pinned to the bytes git stores. On a
# machine with `core.autocrlf=true` a fresh checkout would otherwise write these
# files back as CRLF and the recorded `content_sha256` / `sha256:` anchors would
# stop recomputing. The gate goes red rather than passing silently - but a
# registry that only verifies on one machine is not a registry.

# Dataset tree. `content_sha256` itself is sha256(`before` + NUL + `after`) over
# the parsed fields (scripts/build-ds2.ts:304, scripts/verify-dataset.ts:780), so
# it does not move with line endings; what does move is `build-ds2.ts --check`,
# which re-emits the tree and compares it byte for byte.
datasets/** -text

# Acceptance evidence: the provenance anchors are sha256 of the file on disk.
artifacts/acceptance/** -text

# Run artefacts that carry their own hash (preregistration, staged-context,
# block-gaps, calibration): same rule, same reason.
artifacts/*.json -text

# Generated contracts: `npm run check:schema` re-emits them and fails on any
# byte difference, so a CRLF checkout would read as a schema drift.
schemas/** -text
